[center][size=5][b]OSF - native animation & scene framework for Starfield.[/b][/size][/center]

[center][b][color=#f08a24]Work in progress - report anything broken[/color][/b][/center]

[line]

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/4187/images/17457/17457-1782620284-856347009.png[/img][/center]
OSF is a native animation and scene engine for Starfield, inspired by [b]OStim [/b]and[b] NAF[/b]. It plays animation on actors, keeps multiple characters in sync, anchors them in the world, controls the camera, and runs whole scenes in a data-driven, json based authoring flow.

You can use it directly in-game: an [b]animation browser[/b] to look through everything installed and play it on yourself or nearby NPCs. Or drive it from other mods: you describe a whole scene in a json data file and OSF runs it at runtime, instead of scripting timings and animation events in papyrus yourself.

The engine itself carries no content; the installer offers two optional starter packs - the game's own animation library, and a small immersion emote pack.

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/4187/images/17457/17457-1782620284-2144773043.png[/img][/center]
[list]
[*]In-game [b]animation browser[/b] ([font=Courier New]F10[/font]): search one unified catalog, pick a cast by clicking actors in the world or from a nearby-actor scan, launch on furniture, drag to orbit the camera, and manage running scenes[/*]
[*][b]Vanilla animation library[/b] (optional install): thousands of Starfield's own clips, humans plus 27 creature species, browsable and playable[/*]
[*]Synced multi-actor [b]GLTF / GLB / .af[/b] playback on one shared clock[/*]
[*]JSON [b]scene graphs -[/b] nodes, cues, actions, branching; no scripting needed[/*]
[*]Engine-native [b]Wwise[/b] audio that rides the game mix, with subtitles[/*]
[*]Actor anchoring, camera/control locks, screen fades, equipment hide/restore[/*]
[*]A [b]Papyrus API[/b] ([font=Courier New]OSF.*[/font]) for quest mods to drive scenes[/*]
[/list]

[center][size=4][b][color=#f08a24]NEW IN 1.5.0[/color][/b][/size][/center]
[list]
[*][b]Partial-body animation masks.[/b] Scene roles can drive the upper body, arms, hands, or lower body while Starfield keeps locomotion running on the rest of the actor, with feathered seams and additive-mode support.[/*]
[*][b]Scene-scoped render props.[/b] Authors can clone an equipped item or fixed form, attach it to an actor node, move it between roles or nodes, and destroy it at an authored handoff. OSF cleans up anything left when the scene ends or a save loads.[/*]
[*][b]Better integration for other mods.[/b] SFSE plugins can receive synchronous native scene begin/end, node, cue, and action callbacks, and native or Papyrus consumers can report the minimum OSF Animation version they require.[/*]
[*][b]Scene policy control.[/b] Partial-body and equipment gestures can preserve equipped weapons, slates, and tools instead of OSF automatically clearing every held item.[/*]
[*][b]Cleaner sound authoring.[/b] Sound roles now describe their real job - voice-channel selection, gender substitution, and subtitle speaker - while audio rides Starfield's listener-centered Wwise mix. PCM/WEM generation and worker/cache limits have additional regression coverage.[/*]
[*][b]Animation stability fixes.[/b] Masked and additive playback now rebinds immediately through actor-3D rebuilds and in-place skeleton mutations; one-shot scenes hold their final frame while stopping instead of flashing back to the first pose.[/*]
[/list]

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/4187/images/17457/17457-1782620464-2115394493.png[/img][/center]
[b]Requires:[/b] [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starfield/mods/3256]Address Library for SFSE[/url] and [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starfield/mods/17711]OSF UI 2.0.0 or newer[/url] (renders the browser, settings menu, and hotkeys - the engine and Papyrus API run without it). On an older OSF UI the browser warns you with an [color=#f08a24]UPDATE[/color] badge.

Install with MO2 or Vortex; the installer will offer the animation library and the emote pack - take both. Then in-game:
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]F10[/font] opens the animation browser - pick something, hit LAUNCH.[/*]
[*]Hotkeys and settings live in OSF UI's settings menu, under [b]OSF Animation[/b].[/*]
[/list]
No OSF UI? The engine still runs headless for content mods; smoke-test from the console (~):
[code]cgf "OSFTest.SoloScene"   ; play a looping test animation
cgf "OSFTest.Stop"        ; stop it[/code]

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/4187/images/17457/17457-1782620464-1671481126.png[/img][/center]
[list]
[*][b]Scene authors:[/b] ship scenes as JSON plus GLB/AF clips. No Papyrus, No scripting. Tag a pack name and your scenes group under it in the browser; tag [font=Courier New]player.emote.*[/font] to place a scene in the Emotes facet.[/*]
[*][b]Quest mods:[/b] drive scenes from Papyrus via the [font=Courier New]OSF.*[/font] API.[/*]
[/list]
Getting Started, the scene schema, and the API are documented on [url=https://github.com/ozooma10/osf-animation]GitHub[/url].

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/4187/images/17457/17457-1782620467-859886682.png[/img][/center]
[list]
[*][b]Deweh[/b] ([url=https://github.com/Deweh/NativeAnimationFrameworkSF]NAF[/url]), the playback/graph base OSF is adapted from[/*]
[*][b]Calaverah[/b] ([url=https://github.com/Calaverah/CALUMI.Animation]CALUMI.Animation[/url]), the .af / skeleton decoder [/*]
[*][b]libxse[/b] ([url=https://github.com/libxse/commonlibsf]CommonLibSF[/url]), the SFSE plugin library OSF is built on [/*]
[*]Plus fastgltf, ozz-animation, zlib, nlohmann/json and miniaudio.[/*]
[/list]

 Source and issue tracker on [url=https://github.com/ozooma10/osf-animation]GitHub[/url], Let me know all your complaints and bugs!
